# CACHE-FV-01, D$ -- SUPERSEDED. Not part of the default suite.
#
# The property this file tries to prove IS proven, by dcache_kind.sby, using
# induction instead of a bounded run. This config is kept only because the
# measurements below are why the method had to change, and because the next
# person to reach for a bounded proof on a cache should see the cost curve
# before spending a day on it.
#
# This route still cannot close. That is not a failure any more; it is the
# reason the other file exists.
#
# Kept because the measurements below are the useful result, and because the
# next person to attempt this should not have to rediscover them.
#
# TWO INDEPENDENT REASONS THIS DOES NOT WORK, both measured 2026-09-03.
#
# 1. The bound cannot be reached. Cumulative abc-bmc3 solve time by step, at
#    the 16 B / 4-line geometry:
#
#        step 15     60 s
#        step 16    235 s
#        step 17    949 s        -> ~4x per step
#
#    The I$ (icache.sby) grew ~1.4x per step over the same range and closed at
#    26 in 14m36s. The difference is the write path: a free 4-bit c_wstrb
#    every cycle, dirty tracking, and the writeback state machine.
#
# 2. The bound was too small to be meaningful anyway. cache_fv.sv covers the
#    sequence the D$ property actually turns on -- a writeback of the tracked
#    word followed by a core read of it. Measured with dcache_cover.sby, that
#    sequence is first reachable at step 28. The previous bound here was 26,
#    i.e. below the depth at which the behaviour being proven can occur at
#    all. A PASS at 26 would have been sound and nearly worthless: it would
#    have proven the property over refills and write hits only, never over an
#    eviction.
#
#    That is the more important of the two findings. Reason 1 is a cost
#    problem; reason 2 means the run was not asking the intended question.
#
# Depth is therefore set to 28 -- the measured minimum that reaches the
# sequence -- rather than to a number that finishes. At ~4x per step from 949 s
# at step 17, step 28 is roughly 4^11 times that. It will not terminate.
#
# WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY CLOSE IT
# Not a longer run. BMC has to replay the whole write-evict-writeback-refill
# sequence from reset every time, and that is what costs 4x a step. The two
# routes that avoid it:
#   - k-induction (sby `mode prove`) with invariants over the tag/dirty state,
#     so the eviction does not have to be reached from reset; or
#   - decomposing the property, e.g. proving writeback correctness from an
#     unconstrained starting state with an invariant that the dirty bit implies
#     the SRAM line matches what the core wrote.
# Both are real work and belong with the P4 coherence proofs, which need the
# same machinery over the same state.
[options]
mode bmc
depth 28
# see icache.sby -- pass,fail would report a counterexample as success
expect pass

[engines]
abc bmc3

[script]
read -sv -DDCACHE cache_fv.sv
read -sv cache.v
read -sv sram_1p_bm.v
prep -top cache_fv -flatten
memory_map
opt -fast

[files]
cache_fv.sv
../../../rtl/cache/cache.v
../../models/sram_1p_bm.v
